 ```
 
+## Pruning executions
+
+A repository keeps every execution it has ever recorded. `RedunBackendDb.prune()` removes executions that are no longer needed, along with the records that only they used:
+
+```py
+from redun import Scheduler
+
+scheduler = Scheduler()
+scheduler.load()
+scheduler.backend.prune(["6ae55d13-...", "c0e7cb04-..."])
+```
+
+Records are shared between executions, so a CallNode, Value or Tag is only removed once no remaining execution refers to it. The cached results of the removed task calls are dropped as well, so a call that no remaining execution made runs again the next time it is evaluated. With a [value store](#optional-value-store), the stored data of every removed value is deleted from it too.
+
 ## Database migration
 
 The redun CLI will automatically initialize and upgrade the redun database in most cases, so users will oftentimes not need to manage database versioning themselves. However, there are certain situations where users may want or need to manage database upgrades explicitly, such as upgrading a central shared PostgreSQL database. Here, we review common commands for inspecting and managing database version upgrades.
